Sony ARW6 decoder: reject damaged tiles instead of crashing, output unchanged.

A tile of 2 to 14 rows is padded to 16 rows and keeps a guard row, which
leaves no row for the low band (low_count 0). The guard synthesis then reads
row 0 of an empty plane in sony_arw6_inv53_axis0_high_leading, and a crafted
file crashes the process. The tile height is checked nowhere else. Such a tile
is now rejected with LIBRAW_EXCEPTION_IO_CORRUPT, so libraw_unpack returns an
error; the low band is also required to be non-empty where it is read.

Coefficients with a shift reach almost 2^34 and were truncated to int32_t;
a coefficient that does not fit is now rejected as corrupt. The sign is
applied by negation instead of v - 2 * v * bit, which overflowed for large v.
Both give the same values as before wherever the old code did not overflow.
The sums in the wavelet synthesis are left as they are: they only overflow
for data no valid file contains, and the result is clamped before the curve.

Against LibRaw 40fb564486681a04eba0318058b0519f4f69eba8, apply with patch -p1.
The source file uses LF line endings.

--- a/src/decoders/sony_arw6.cpp
+++ b/src/decoders/sony_arw6.cpp
@@ -719,7 +719,14 @@
     uint32_t x = nbits > 0 ? br.read_bits(nbits) : 0;
     int32_t v = int32_t(x);
     if (shift > 0 && x > 0)
-      v = int32_t(((uint64_t(2) * x + 1) << (shift - 1)) - (x & 1));
+    {
+      // With up to 19 bits and a shift of up to 15 the magnitude reaches
+      // almost 2^34. A value that does not fit in int32_t cannot come from a
+      // valid file; reject it instead of truncating it.
+      const uint64_t m = ((uint64_t(2) * x + 1) << (shift - 1)) - (x & 1);
+      sony_arw6_require(m <= uint64_t(INT32_MAX));
+      v = int32_t(m);
+    }
     vals[i] = v;
   }
 }
@@ -729,8 +736,9 @@
   for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++)
     if (vals[i] > 0)
     {
-      const uint32_t bit = br.read_bits(1);
-      vals[i] = vals[i] - 2 * vals[i] * int32_t(bit);
+      // Same result as v - 2 * v * bit, without the overflow of 2 * v.
+      if (br.read_bits(1))
+        vals[i] = -vals[i];
     }
 }
 
